            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Man invested $45M in a Nicolas Cage movie. It got stolen off Netflix’s desk.</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/07/man-invested-45m-in-a-nicolas-cage-movie-it-got-stolen-off-netflixs-desk/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/07/man-invested-45m-in-a-nicolas-cage-movie-it-got-stolen-off-netflixs-desk/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 31 Jul 2026 16:17:55 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[movie streaming]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Netflix]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[piracy]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/07/man-invested-45m-in-a-nicolas-cage-movie-it-got-stolen-off-netflixs-desk/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Filmmaker sues Netflix over stolen screener of unreleased Nicolas Cage movie.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Netflix has been sued after admitting that an unencrypted drive containing an unreleased, hotly anticipated 2026 blockbuster starring Nicolas Cage was stolen from its offices.</p>
<p>In a <a href="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Op-Fortitude-v-Netflix-Complaint-7-29-26.pdf">lawsuit</a> filed Wednesday, Netflix was accused of waiting a week to warn the production company, Op-Fortitude, that the movie, <em>Fortitude</em>, was swiped, along with other drives that were “stolen right off a Netflix desk.”</p>
<p>“Someone stole a good amount of drives from our office desks this past week,” Sean Berney, director of Netflix Original Film, said in an email to a production company representative after several attempts to get Netflix to return the drives were allegedly ignored.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/07/man-invested-45m-in-a-nicolas-cage-movie-it-got-stolen-off-netflixs-desk/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/07/man-invested-45m-in-a-nicolas-cage-movie-it-got-stolen-off-netflixs-desk/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>150</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content height="648" medium="image" type="image/jpeg" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/GettyImages-2275512221-1024x648.jpg" width="1024">
<media:thumbnail height="500" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/GettyImages-2275512221-500x500.jpg" width="500"/>
<media:credit>Kristina Bumphrey / Contributor | Variety</media:credit><media:text>Nicolas Cage stars in Fortitude as the real-life spy who inspired James Bond.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>AI scammers outperform humans when it comes to building trust</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/07/ai-scammers-outperform-humans-when-it-comes-to-building-trust/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/07/ai-scammers-outperform-humans-when-it-comes-to-building-trust/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Andy Greenberg, wired.com]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 31 Jul 2026 14:01:40 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Security]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI agents]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[pig butchering]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[scams]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[syndication]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/07/ai-scammers-outperform-humans-when-it-comes-to-building-trust/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[The AI chatbot was more effective at creating “exploitable trust” than the humans.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>The notion that <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/he-leaked-the-secrets-southeast-asian-scam-compound-then-had-to-get-out-alive/">scammers</a> can use <a href="https://www.wired.com/tag/artificial-intelligence/">AI</a> to sharpen their deceptions, polish their language, and lubricate their banter with victims is now a reality for anyone fighting the fraud operations that steal tens of billions of dollars a year worldwide. But can AI fully <i>replace</i> a human scammer, autonomously building the web of deception leading up to the fake investment that defrauds the mark? One study's experiment suggests that it can—and may even be able to carry out the majority of that long con more effectively than humans.</p>
<p>Researchers from four universities—Amrita Vishwa Vidyapeetham in India, Foscari University of Venice, the University of Melbourne, and Ben Gurion University of the Negev—carried out a broad study on the use and potential of generative AI chatbots in the growing scam industry centered around a form of fraud known as “<a href="https://www.wired.com/story/pig-butchering-scam-invasion/">pig butchering</a>,” text-based romance scams that eventually shift to fake crypto investments that steal as much as six-figure sums from victims. In their study, the researchers pitted AI chatbots directly against humans in a simulation of the scamming process—or more specifically, the long, trust-building conversations that eventually lead up to soliciting a fake investment from the scam’s target.</p>
<p>They found that for the relationship-establishing stages of the scam—the stage that in real-world scams typically represents the longest part of the interactions with the victim, often stretching to months—an AI chatbot performed remarkably effectively, successfully impersonating a human and by some measures outperforming the real human “scammers” in their experiment.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/07/ai-scammers-outperform-humans-when-it-comes-to-building-trust/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/07/ai-scammers-outperform-humans-when-it-comes-to-building-trust/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>54</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content height="648" medium="image" type="image/jpeg" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/scammer-1-1152x648.jpg" width="1152">
<media:thumbnail height="500" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/scammer-1-500x500.jpg" width="500"/>
<media:credit>Nansan Houn/Getty</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Pathogenic review: Damn, it feels good to be a virus</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/07/pathogenic-review-damn-it-feels-good-to-be-a-virus/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/07/pathogenic-review-damn-it-feels-good-to-be-a-virus/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Aaron Zimmerman]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 31 Jul 2026 13:00:18 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[Gaming]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[immune system]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Indie games]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[pathogenic]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[roguelike]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[roguelite]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[twin-stick shooter]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/07/pathogenic-review-damn-it-feels-good-to-be-a-virus/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[The hot new roguelite twin-stick shooter lives up to the hype.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Over the past couple of weeks, my immune system has been struggling to fight off a cold. It’s been unpleasant, as all colds are. But throughout this time—in defiance of my plight—I’ve been waging my own war against immune systems, thanks to <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/3808690/Pathogenic/"><em>Pathogenic</em></a>, a new <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2025/12/please-send-help-i-cant-stop-playing-these-roguelikes/">roguelite</a> twin-stick shooter from developer Aberrant Labs.</p>
<p>As I've downed endless shots of ineffective cough syrup, I’ve done little else with my free time than repeatedly take on the role of a bodily invader. And let me tell you: Being on the enemy’s side is much more fun. Really fun, as it turns out.</p>
<p>Action roguelites are a dime a dozen these days, and it takes something special to stand out. So as quiet Internet whispers turned into widespread cacophonous praise—the little indie game quickly racked up 100,000 sales and an “overwhelmingly positive” review score on Steam—I set aside my default skepticism and gave it a shot.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/07/pathogenic-review-damn-it-feels-good-to-be-a-virus/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/07/pathogenic-review-damn-it-feels-good-to-be-a-virus/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>40</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content height="648" medium="image" type="image/png" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/pathogenic1-1152x648.png" width="1152">
<media:thumbnail height="500" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/pathogenic1-500x500.png" width="500"/>
<media:credit>Aberrant Labs/Slug Disco</media:credit><media:text>In &lt;em&gt;Pathogenic&lt;/em&gt;, you are the disease.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
